  "Name": "Hass1959-Fig18-n16-on-Ti-4360A",
  "Source": "G. Hass and R. Tousey, \"Reflecting Coatings for the Extreme Ultraviolet,\" J. Opt. Soc. Am. 49, 593-602 (1959). doi:10.1364/JOSA.49.000593 - Sec. VI p. 601 text and Fig. 18 caption/left panel (Ti: n = 2.04, k = 2.85 at 4360 A; film n = 1.6)",
  "Notes": "Counter-example in Fig. 18 (left): a nonabsorbing n = 1.6 film on titanium at 4360 A produces a deep reflectance minimum instead of enhancement; thickness set at the plotted minimum\nSpecification: Calculated reflectance of a nonabsorbing n = 1.6 film on Ti at 4360 A versus film optical thickness.\nReported: Fig. 18 left (calculated, normal incidence, 4360 A): uncoated Ti R = 53 % (text p. 601: 'a reflectance of uncoated titanium of 53%'; exact from n = 2.04, k = 2.85 is 53.0 %); deep broad minimum ~22 % near n*d ~ 0.2 lambda; narrow shallow region of enhanced reflection (~56 %) near n*d ~ 0.43-0.45 lambda; back to 53 % at 0.5 lambda. Independent check by me: R = 21.3 % at n*d = 0.2 lambda (this design), minimum 21.1 % at 0.19 lambda, maximum 55.7 % at 0.44-0.45 lambda.\nNotes: Thickness 0.8 QWOT (n*d = 0.2 lambda = 87.2 nm optical, 54.5 nm physical at 436 nm) chosen by me at the plotted minimum; the figure is a thickness sweep (0-2 QWOT). Ti treated as an opaque substrate. The film is a generic n = 1.6, k = 0 dielectric (paper: 'a film of n = 1.6').\nLayers from Sec. VI p. 601 text and Fig. 18 caption/left panel (Ti: n = 2.04, k = 2.85 at 4360 A; film n = 1.6); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R 21.3 % at 436 nm; paper R ~22 % (calculated, read from Fig. 18).",
